WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Next, we'll be covering seizures, but before we get into the specifics, I want to show

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.160
you a short video of what a seizure actually looks like in a real world setting.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.100
It can be a little unsettling to watch someone lose control of their body, but seeing this

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.320
now is one of the best ways to make sure you don't freeze up if you ever witness it in

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.320
person.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.160
While you watch, I want you to look closely at the person's movements and their level

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.340
of awareness.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.500
You might see the classic shaking or jerky movements, but you might also notice moments

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.820
where the person is just very still or has a glassy-eyed stare.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.439
Just take it in for a moment, and then we will move to the next slide to talk about

00:00:37.439 --> 00:00:43.820
exactly what is happening in the brain and how you can step in to help.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:49.000
A seizure can be a very scary thing to witness, but once you understand what is happening,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.020
you can stay calm and take the right steps to keep the person safe.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.480
A seizure is a sudden surge of electricity in the brain.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.279
It causes the brain to send out mixed-up signals, which can make a person act in ways

00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:04.080
they can't control for a short time.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.919
When you think of a seizure, you probably imagine someone shaking or falling to the

00:01:07.919 --> 00:01:14.000
ground, and that definitely happens when someone loses muscle control or has jerky movements.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.320
However, seizures can also look the exact opposite.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:24.040
A person might just get very still, have a glassy-eyed stare, and become completely unresponsive.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.800
Seizures can be triggered by things like epilepsy, head injuries, low blood sugar,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.680
or even cardiac arrest.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.099
For those of you who work with children, you might encounter febrile seizures, which happen

00:01:35.099 --> 00:01:39.839
in younger kids when their body temperature rises too quickly from a fever.

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:44.639
If you witness someone actively seizing, first make sure someone calls 911.

00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:48.559
Then move any furniture or sharp objects out of the way so they don't strike them during

00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:49.959
jerky movements.

00:01:49.959 --> 00:01:54.919
If you can, place something soft like a pillow or a folded jacket under their head to protect

00:01:54.919 --> 00:01:56.519
it from the hard floor.

00:01:56.519 --> 00:01:59.519
It is vital that you never put anything in their mouth.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:04.220
Not only is it a major choking hazard for them, but you also risk a serious injury to

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:05.639
your own hand.

00:02:05.639 --> 00:02:11.399
Finally, once the shaking stops, gently roll them onto their side into the recovery position

00:02:11.399 --> 00:02:14.039
to keep their airway clear until help arrives.

